"{\"eval_set_result_id\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640733.0696387\",\"eval_set_result_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640733.0696387\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_case_results\":[{\"eval_set_file\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_id\":\"Test2\",\"final_eval_status\":2,\"eval_metric_results\":null,\"overall_eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":1.0,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":0.7,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.6011778563015313,\"eval_status\":2,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}],\"eval_metric_result_per_invocation\":[{\"actual_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-e558685b-0159-467d-8417-803eedfb8189\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"ITINERARY: ## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto: Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary is structured to group activities by geographic location to minimize travel time, maximizing your immersion in Kyoto's temples, traditions, and natural beauty.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Suggested Accommodation Area\\n\\nFor a cultural deep-dive, consider staying in the **Higashiyama (Eastern Kyoto)** or **Central Kyoto (near Kawaramachi/Gion)** districts. These areas offer excellent access to historic sites, dining, and a wonderful atmosphere for evening walks.\\n\\n### Estimated Budget Summary (Per Person, Excluding Flights)\\n\\n| Expense Category | Estimated Daily Cost (JPY) | Estimated Total (7 Days, JPY) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation** (Mid-range hotel/Ryokan) | \u00a515,000 \u2013 \u00a525,000 | \u00a5105,000 \u2013 \u00a5175,000 | Varies significantly based on quality and season. |\\n| **Food & Dining** (Including one nice meal) | \u00a56,000 \u2013 \u00a510,000 | \u00a542,000 \u2013 \u00a570,000 | Assumes a mix of quick lunches and moderate dinners. |\\n| **Local Transport** (Bus/Train/IC Card use) | \u00a51,000 \u2013 \u00a51,500 | \u00a57,000 \u2013 \u00a510,500 | Includes potential bus day passes. |\\n| **Entrance Fees** (Temples/Museums) | \u00a52,000 \u2013 \u00a53,000 | \u00a514,000 \u2013 \u00a521,000 | Based on visiting 2-3 paid sites daily. |\\n| **Experiences** (Tea Ceremony, etc.) | N/A (Budgeted below) | \u00a510,000 \u2013 \u00a515,000 | Estimate for one structured experience. |\\n| **Contingency/Shopping** | \u00a53,000+ | \u00a521,000+ | For souvenirs, snacks, and incidentals. |\\n| **TOTAL ESTIMATE** | **\u00a527,000 \u2013 \u00a540,000+** | **\u00a5199,000 \u2013 \u00a5291,500+** | *(Approx. $1,300 - $1,900 USD based on current exchange rates)* |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Detailed Daily Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & Golden Zenith (North/West Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Arrival, Check-in, & Travel Setup | 3 hours | Settle into accommodation. Purchase an ICOCA card if you don't have one. |\\n| Lunch | Local Udon/Soba near your hotel | 1 hour | Quick, comforting start. |\\n| Afternoon | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | 1.5 hours | Visit mid-afternoon for good light, but note crowds are highest here. |\\n| Late Afternoon | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | 1.5 hours | Contemplate the famous Zen rock garden. Perfect contrast to Kinkaku-ji\u2019s opulence. |\\n| Evening | Dinner in Central Kyoto (e.g., Pontocho Alley) | 2.5 hours | **Dining:** Explore Pontocho for atmospheric dining along the Kamo River (reservations recommended). |\\n| **Transport Focus** | Primarily Bus (from Central Kyoto to Kinkaku-ji/Ryoan-ji and back). | | |\\n\\n### Day 2: Arashiyama Bamboo & Zen Gardens (West Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Early Morning | Travel to Arashiyama & **Bamboo Grove** | 3 hours (incl. travel) | **Crucial: Arrive before 8:30 AM** to experience the grove without massive crowds. |\\n| Late Morning | **Tenryu-ji Temple** | 2 hours | Explore the vast, stunning UNESCO gardens. |\\n| Lunch | **Shojin Ryori (Zen Cuisine)** | 1.5 hours | **Dining:** Try *Shigetsu* within Tenryu-ji for an authentic vegetarian temple lunch experience (book ahead). |\\n| Afternoon | **Togetsukyo Bridge & River Walk** | 1.5 hours | Relaxing nature walk along the Katsura River. |\\n| Late Afternoon | Return to Kyoto & Relaxation | 1 hour | Travel back to your base area. |\\n| Evening | Dinner in Nishijin or near your hotel | 2 hours | **Dining:** Explore local neighborhood eateries for Yakitori or casual Japanese fare. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | JR Sagano Line or Randen Tram to Arashiyama. | | |\\n\\n### Day 3: Thousand Gates & Autumnal Grandeur (South Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Very Early AM | Travel to **Fushimi Inari Taisha** | 3 hours (incl. travel/hike) | **Crucial: Arrive before 7:30 AM.** Hike the torii gates trail as far as you feel comfortable for solitude. |\\n| Late Morning | Descend & Explore the lower shrine areas | 1 hour | Purchase an *omamori* (charm) or try the Fox-shaped fortune slips. |\\n| Lunch | Inari Area Street Food | 1 hour | **Dining:** Try *Inari Sushi* (rice stuffed tofu pockets) or grilled sparrows (if adventurous) near the shrine entrance. |\\n| Afternoon | **Tofuku-ji Temple** | 2 hours | Famous for its massive Sanmon Gate and beautiful, sweeping views (especially autumn leaves). |\\n| Late Afternoon | Travel & Tea Prep | 1.5 hours | Head back toward Central Kyoto. Prepare for your cultural deep-dive tomorrow. |\\n| Evening | Dinner in Gion area | 2.5 hours | **Dining:** Look for a modest restaurant in Gion, perhaps trying *obanzai* (Kyoto home cooking). Be respectful if spotting Geiko/Maiko. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | JR Nara Line to Fushimi Inari and Tofuku-ji. | | |\\n\\n### Day 4: Deep Dive into Tradition & Art (Central/East Kyoto Preparation)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Tea Ceremony Experience (*Chado*)** | 2 hours | **Activity Focus:** Book a highly rated, structured ceremony in advance (e.g., in the Gion or Higashiyama area). Focus on etiquette. |\\n| Late Morning | **Kennin-ji Temple** | 1.5 hours | Kyoto\u2019s oldest Zen temple. Admire the twin dragon ceiling painting in the Dharma Hall. |\\n| Lunch | Near Kennin-ji/Gion | 1 hour | **Dining:** A light lunch or coffee/tea break to digest the morning\u2019s ceremony. |\\n| Afternoon | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple** | 1.5 hours | Awe-inspiring hall housing 1001 Kannon statues. Photography is strictly prohibited inside. |\\n| Late Afternoon | Museum/Gallery Visit (Optional) | 1.5 hours | If time allows, visit the Kyoto National Museum nearby for context on the art you\u2019ve seen. |\\n| Evening | Explore Nishiki Market (for pre-dinner grazing) & Dinner | 3 hours | **Dining:** Explore **Nishiki Market** (closes around 5-6 PM for shopping, but nearby restaurants stay open). Find dinner in the surrounding Kawaramachi area. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | Mostly walking and short bus hops within Central/Southern Kyoto. | | |\\n\\n### Day 5: Eastern Hills, History, and Views (Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Early Morning | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | 2 hours | Arrive early (opens at 6 AM) to enjoy the famous wooden stage before the large crowds arrive. |\\n| Late Morning | **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka Streets** | 2 hours | Wander and shop these beautifully preserved, historic cobblestone streets leading up to/down from Kiyomizu-dera. Look for Kiyomizu-yaki pottery. |\\n| Lunch | Near Kodai-ji or Yasaka Shrine | 1 hour | **Dining:** Grab street snacks (like *yatsuhashi* sweets) or a small caf\u00e9 lunch. |\\n| Afternoon | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | 1.5 hours | Visit this Zen temple known for its stunning sand garden and moss garden. |\\n| Late Afternoon | **Philosopher\u2019s Path (Tetsugaku no Michi)** | 2 hours | Enjoy a contemplative walk south along the canal from Ginkaku-ji. |\\n| Evening | Dinner near the Path or back in Gion | 2.5 hours | **Dining:** Look for high-quality Tofu or *Kaiseki* (if budget permits) to reflect the refined nature of Higashiyama. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | Bus to Kiyomizu-dera, then walking between all sites today. | | |\\n\\n### Day 6: Mountain Spirits & Forest Hikes (Optional Day Trip: Kurama/Kibune)\\n\\n*This day requires slightly more travel time but offers a significant nature and spiritual contrast to the city.*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Travel to Kurama via Eiden Railway | 1.5 hours | Take the scenic Eiden Line north from Demachiyanagi Station. |\\n| Late Morning | **Kurama-dera Temple** | 2 hours | Hike up the steep path (or take the cable car) to this mountain temple, associated with Tengu folklore. |\\n| Lunch | Kibune Area | 1.5 hours | **Dining:** Head down the mountain trail toward Kibune. In summer, try *Kawadoko* dining (platforms built over the river). |\\n| Afternoon | **Kifune Shrine** | 2 hours | Famous for its striking red staircase and water fortune-telling slips (*Omikuji*). A lovely, atmospheric shrine. |\\n| Late Afternoon | Return Journey to Kyoto | 1.5 hours | Relaxing train ride back to the city. |\\n| Evening | Farewell Dinner - Splurge Option | 3 hours | **Dining:** Choose a special Kyoto cuisine you haven't tried yet, perhaps high-end *Wagyu* or *Kaiseki* to celebrate the trip. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | Eiden Railway (use ICOCA or a dedicated day pass). Walking/hiking between Kurama and Kibune. | | |\\n\\n### Day 7: Reflection & Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Last Temple Visit or Shopping | 2 hours | **Suggestion:** Revisit a favorite spot briefly, or visit a smaller site like **Chion-in Temple** (massive Sanmon gate). |\\n| Late Morning | Souvenir Shopping & Post Office | 1.5 hours | Final chance for *matcha*, artisanal goods, or sending postcards. |\\n| Lunch | Quick, favorite Kyoto meal | 1 hour | **Dining:** Your favorite simple meal from the week (e.g., ramen, tempura). |\\n| Afternoon | Travel to Airport/Next Destination | Varies | Ensure adequate time for travel to Kansai (KIX) or Itami (ITM) airports, typically requiring the Haruka Express or Limousine Bus from Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | IC Card usage for final transit. | | |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### OPTIMIZATION TIPS: Money-Saving & Budget Alternatives\\n\\n1.  **Transportation Savings:**\\n    *   **Kyoto City Bus All-Day Pass (\u00a5700):** If you plan to take 3 or more bus rides in a single day, this pass saves money over single fares (which are usually \u00a5230). This is highly useful on Days 1, 3, and 5.\\n    *   **Walk More:** Districts like Higashiyama (Day 5) are best explored on foot. Grouping temples geographically (as the itinerary does) minimizes transit costs significantly.\\n    *   **Avoid Taxis:** Use taxis only late at night or when carrying heavy luggage.\\n\\n2.  **Dining & Food Budget:**\\n    *   **Convenience Store Breakfasts:** Stock up on *onigiri* (rice balls), fresh fruit, and excellent pastries at 7-Eleven or Lawson for breakfast instead of paying caf\u00e9 prices (Saves approx. \u00a5800\u2013\u00a51,500 daily).\\n    *   **Supermarket Deals:** Visit local grocery stores (like Life or Aeon) just before closing for discounted ready-made meals (*bento* boxes) for an evening meal.\\n    *   **Lunch over Dinner:** Eat your main, most expensive meal at lunchtime. Many high-end restaurants offer significantly cheaper lunch sets (e.g., *Kaiseki* lunch vs. dinner).\\n    *   **Temple Lunch:** The *Shojin Ryori* at Tenryu-ji (Day 2) is a cultural experience but can be pricier. **Budget Alternative:** Seek out local *Yudofu* (boiled tofu) restaurants in Arashiyama, which offer satisfying, traditional, and usually more affordable vegetarian options.\\n\\n3.  **Entrance Fees:**\\n    *   **Free Sites:** Prioritize free sites like Fushimi Inari Taisha, Kifune Shrine, and the Philosopher's Path.\\n    *   **Temple Hopping Discount:** If you find yourself visiting many temples in one area (like Higashiyama), some temples offer combined tickets or loyalty stamp cards that can offer minor savings over time.\\n\\n### TRAVEL ESSENTIALS: Packing and Preparation Advice\\n\\n1.  **Footwear:** This is the most critical item. You will walk **miles** daily, often on uneven cobblestones (Higashiyama) or steep paths (Fushimi Inari, Kurama). Pack extremely comfortable, broken-in walking shoes.\\n2.  **Layered Clothing:** Kyoto weather, especially during shoulder seasons (Spring/Autumn), changes rapidly. Bring light layers: a base layer, a mid-layer (sweater/fleece), and a waterproof/windproof outer shell.\\n3.  **Temple Visit Essentials:**\\n    *   **Small Towel/Handkerchief:** For wiping sweat, especially in humidity, as many public restrooms do not provide paper towels.\\n    *   **Socks:** For Day 4 (Tea Ceremony) and any Ryokan stay, ensure you have clean, hole-free socks, as you must remove shoes frequently.\\n4.  **Weather Specific (If traveling in early Spring/late Autumn):** Bring a light scarf and gloves, as mornings and evenings can be chilly, even if the midday sun is warm.\\n5.  **Power:** Bring a Type A or B plug adapter (standard North American style) and a portable power bank, as you will use your phone heavily for navigation and photos.\\n\\n### BACKUP PLANS: Weather & Unexpected Situations\\n\\n1.  **Heavy Rain/Typhoon:**\\n    *   **Substitute Day 5/Day 1:** Move the outdoor-heavy Day 5 (Higashiyama walking) indoors.\\n    *   **Indoor Alternatives:** Utilize the **Kyoto National Museum** (near Sanjusangendo), the **Kyoto International Manga Museum** (near Karasuma Oike), or focus extra time on the large indoor halls of temples like Sanjusangendo or Tofukuji.\\n    *   **Shopping Arcades:** Spend time walking through the covered arcades around Kawaramachi/Shijo Dori.\\n\\n2.  **Overcrowding (Especially Fushimi Inari/Kinkaku-ji):**\\n    *   **Flexibility:** If a site is unbearably busy upon arrival, pivot immediately. Go further up the Fushimi Inari trail until the crowds thin out, or visit Ryoan-ji first (Day 1) and return to Kinkaku-ji later in the day when mid-day tour buses have departed.\\n    *   **Early Start Backup:** The itinerary emphasizes very early starts for a reason. If you wake up late, swap that day\u2019s schedule with a more flexible day (like Day 4).\\n\\n3.  **Kurama/Kibune Closure (Day 6 Backup):**\\n    *   If the Eiden line is disrupted (rare, but possible due to heavy snow or storm damage), substitute with a trip to **Nara** (as suggested in the original research) to see the Great Buddha and interact with the deer, which is easily accessible via the JR Nara Line from Kyoto Station.\\n\\n### LOCAL APPS, WEBSITES, & RESOURCES\\n\\n1.  **Transportation & Navigation:**\\n    *   **Google Maps:** Essential for real-time public transport routing, showing exact bus numbers, train platforms, and walking directions in Japan.\\n    *   **Japan Travel by NAVITIME:** Excellent alternative for complex train transfers or exploring alternative routes if Google Maps seems overly complicated.\\n2.  **Communication & Language:**\\n    *   **Google Translate (Offline Japanese Pack):** Download the Japanese language pack so you can use the camera function to translate signs, menus, and labels without relying on Wi-Fi.\\n3.  **IC Card Management:**\\n    *   If using a digital wallet (Apple Pay/Google Pay) on your phone, you can manage and top up your **ICOCA** card digitally, saving time queuing at station ticket machines.\\n4.  **Booking Experiences:**\\n    *   **Klook/Voyagin:** Useful for pre-booking structured cultural experiences like the **Tea Ceremony** (Day 4) or special dining reservations (like *Kawadoko* dining on Day 6), often providing English-language booking support.\\n\\n### CULTURAL DO'S & DON'TS\\n\\n| DO (Respectful Travel) | DON'T (Avoid Offense) |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **DO** accept items (like business cards or change) with both hands. | **DON'T** eat or drink while walking in public (outside of designated festival/street food stalls). |\\n| **DO** learn a few basic phrases: *Arigato gozaimasu* (Thank you very much), *Sumimasen* (Excuse me/Sorry). | **DON'T** walk through the central aisles of temples or shrines; stick to the side paths unless instructed otherwise. |\\n| **DO** wait patiently in lines; queuing is orderly and expected. | **DON'T** point directly at people, especially *Geiko* or *Maiko* in Gion. Observe from a respectful distance. |\\n| **DO** remove your shoes before entering homes, traditional restaurants, temples, and sometimes certain museum areas. Look for shoe shelves (*getabako*). | **DON'T** use your chopsticks to point, pass food to another person's chopsticks, or stick them upright in your rice bowl (this mimics funeral rites). |\\n| **DO** use your ICOCA card/cash by placing it neatly on the small tray provided at counters, rather than handing it directly to the clerk. | **DON'T** use flash photography inside temples or museums, or where signs explicitly forbid it. |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"invocation_events\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640714.752035,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":{\"agent_details\":{\"DestinationResearchAgent\":{\"name\":\"DestinationResearchAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a travel researcher. You will be given a destination and travel preferences, and you will research:\\n    - Best time to visit and weather patterns\\n    - Top attractions and must-see locations\\n    - Local culture, customs, and etiquette tips\\n    - Transportation options within the destination\\n    - Safety considerations and travel requirements\\n    Provide comprehensive destination insights for trip planning.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"DestinationResearchAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that researches travel destinations and gathers essential information\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[{\"function_declarations\":null,\"retrieval\":null,\"google_search_retrieval\":null,\"computer_use\":null,\"file_search\":null,\"code_execution\":null,\"enterprise_web_search\":null,\"google_maps\":null,\"google_search\":{\"exclude_domains\":null,\"blocking_confidence\":null,\"time_range_filter\":null},\"url_context\":null}]},\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\":{\"name\":\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a professional travel planner. Using the research from \\\"destination_research\\\" output, create a detailed itinerary that includes:\\n    - Day-by-day schedule with recommended activities\\n    - Suggested accommodation areas or districts\\n    - Estimated time requirements for each activity\\n    - Meal recommendations and dining suggestions\\n    - Budget estimates for major expenses\\n    Structure it logically for easy following during the trip.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that creates structured travel itineraries with daily schedules\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]},\"TravelOptimizerAgent\":{\"name\":\"TravelOptimizerAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a seasoned travel consultant. Using the itinerary from \\\"travel_itinerary\\\" output, optimize it by adding:\\n    - Money-saving tips and budget alternatives\\n    - Packing recommendations specific to the destination\\n    - Backup plans for weather or unexpected situations\\n    - Local apps, websites, or resources to download\\n    - Cultural do's and don'ts for respectful travel\\n\\n    Format the final output as:\\n    \\n    ITINERARY: ## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto: Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary is structured to group activities by geographic location to minimize travel time, maximizing your immersion in Kyoto's temples, traditions, and natural beauty.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Suggested Accommodation Area\\n\\nFor a cultural deep-dive, consider staying in the **Higashiyama (Eastern Kyoto)** or **Central Kyoto (near Kawaramachi/Gion)** districts. These areas offer excellent access to historic sites, dining, and a wonderful atmosphere for evening walks.\\n\\n### Estimated Budget Summary (Per Person, Excluding Flights)\\n\\n| Expense Category | Estimated Daily Cost (JPY) | Estimated Total (7 Days, JPY) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation** (Mid-range hotel/Ryokan) | \u00a515,000 \u2013 \u00a525,000 | \u00a5105,000 \u2013 \u00a5175,000 | Varies significantly based on quality and season. |\\n| **Food & Dining** (Including one nice meal) | \u00a56,000 \u2013 \u00a510,000 | \u00a542,000 \u2013 \u00a570,000 | Assumes a mix of quick lunches and moderate dinners. |\\n| **Local Transport** (Bus/Train/IC Card use) | \u00a51,000 \u2013 \u00a51,500 | \u00a57,000 \u2013 \u00a510,500 | Includes potential bus day passes. |\\n| **Entrance Fees** (Temples/Museums) | \u00a52,000 \u2013 \u00a53,000 | \u00a514,000 \u2013 \u00a521,000 | Based on visiting 2-3 paid sites daily. |\\n| **Experiences** (Tea Ceremony, etc.) | N/A (Budgeted below) | \u00a510,000 \u2013 \u00a515,000 | Estimate for one structured experience. |\\n| **Contingency/Shopping** | \u00a53,000+ | \u00a521,000+ | For souvenirs, snacks, and incidentals. |\\n| **TOTAL ESTIMATE** | **\u00a527,000 \u2013 \u00a540,000+** | **\u00a5199,000 \u2013 \u00a5291,500+** | *(Approx. $1,300 - $1,900 USD based on current exchange rates)* |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Detailed Daily Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & Golden Zenith (North/West Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Arrival, Check-in, & Travel Setup | 3 hours | Settle into accommodation. Purchase an ICOCA card if you don't have one. |\\n| Lunch | Local Udon/Soba near your hotel | 1 hour | Quick, comforting start. |\\n| Afternoon | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | 1.5 hours | Visit mid-afternoon for good light, but note crowds are highest here. |\\n| Late Afternoon | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | 1.5 hours | Contemplate the famous Zen rock garden. Perfect contrast to Kinkaku-ji\u2019s opulence. |\\n| Evening | Dinner in Central Kyoto (e.g., Pontocho Alley) | 2.5 hours | **Dining:** Explore Pontocho for atmospheric dining along the Kamo River (reservations recommended). |\\n| **Transport Focus** | Primarily Bus (from Central Kyoto to Kinkaku-ji/Ryoan-ji and back). | | |\\n\\n### Day 2: Arashiyama Bamboo & Zen Gardens (West Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Early Morning | Travel to Arashiyama & **Bamboo Grove** | 3 hours (incl. travel) | **Crucial: Arrive before 8:30 AM** to experience the grove without massive crowds. |\\n| Late Morning | **Tenryu-ji Temple** | 2 hours | Explore the vast, stunning UNESCO gardens. |\\n| Lunch | **Shojin Ryori (Zen Cuisine)** | 1.5 hours | **Dining:** Try *Shigetsu* within Tenryu-ji for an authentic vegetarian temple lunch experience (book ahead). |\\n| Afternoon | **Togetsukyo Bridge & River Walk** | 1.5 hours | Relaxing nature walk along the Katsura River. |\\n| Late Afternoon | Return to Kyoto & Relaxation | 1 hour | Travel back to your base area. |\\n| Evening | Dinner in Nishijin or near your hotel | 2 hours | **Dining:** Explore local neighborhood eateries for Yakitori or casual Japanese fare. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | JR Sagano Line or Randen Tram to Arashiyama. | | |\\n\\n### Day 3: Thousand Gates & Autumnal Grandeur (South Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Very Early AM | Travel to **Fushimi Inari Taisha** | 3 hours (incl. travel/hike) | **Crucial: Arrive before 7:30 AM.** Hike the torii gates trail as far as you feel comfortable for solitude. |\\n| Late Morning | Descend & Explore the lower shrine areas | 1 hour | Purchase an *omamori* (charm) or try the Fox-shaped fortune slips. |\\n| Lunch | Inari Area Street Food | 1 hour | **Dining:** Try *Inari Sushi* (rice stuffed tofu pockets) or grilled sparrows (if adventurous) near the shrine entrance. |\\n| Afternoon | **Tofuku-ji Temple** | 2 hours | Famous for its massive Sanmon Gate and beautiful, sweeping views (especially autumn leaves). |\\n| Late Afternoon | Travel & Tea Prep | 1.5 hours | Head back toward Central Kyoto. Prepare for your cultural deep-dive tomorrow. |\\n| Evening | Dinner in Gion area | 2.5 hours | **Dining:** Look for a modest restaurant in Gion, perhaps trying *obanzai* (Kyoto home cooking). Be respectful if spotting Geiko/Maiko. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | JR Nara Line to Fushimi Inari and Tofuku-ji. | | |\\n\\n### Day 4: Deep Dive into Tradition & Art (Central/East Kyoto Preparation)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Tea Ceremony Experience (*Chado*)** | 2 hours | **Activity Focus:** Book a highly rated, structured ceremony in advance (e.g., in the Gion or Higashiyama area). Focus on etiquette. |\\n| Late Morning | **Kennin-ji Temple** | 1.5 hours | Kyoto\u2019s oldest Zen temple. Admire the twin dragon ceiling painting in the Dharma Hall. |\\n| Lunch | Near Kennin-ji/Gion | 1 hour | **Dining:** A light lunch or coffee/tea break to digest the morning\u2019s ceremony. |\\n| Afternoon | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple** | 1.5 hours | Awe-inspiring hall housing 1001 Kannon statues. Photography is strictly prohibited inside. |\\n| Late Afternoon | Museum/Gallery Visit (Optional) | 1.5 hours | If time allows, visit the Kyoto National Museum nearby for context on the art you\u2019ve seen. |\\n| Evening | Explore Nishiki Market (for pre-dinner grazing) & Dinner | 3 hours | **Dining:** Explore **Nishiki Market** (closes around 5-6 PM for shopping, but nearby restaurants stay open). Find dinner in the surrounding Kawaramachi area. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | Mostly walking and short bus hops within Central/Southern Kyoto. | | |\\n\\n### Day 5: Eastern Hills, History, and Views (Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Early Morning | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | 2 hours | Arrive early (opens at 6 AM) to enjoy the famous wooden stage before the large crowds arrive. |\\n| Late Morning | **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka Streets** | 2 hours | Wander and shop these beautifully preserved, historic cobblestone streets leading up to/down from Kiyomizu-dera. Look for Kiyomizu-yaki pottery. |\\n| Lunch | Near Kodai-ji or Yasaka Shrine | 1 hour | **Dining:** Grab street snacks (like *yatsuhashi* sweets) or a small caf\u00e9 lunch. |\\n| Afternoon | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | 1.5 hours | Visit this Zen temple known for its stunning sand garden and moss garden. |\\n| Late Afternoon | **Philosopher\u2019s Path (Tetsugaku no Michi)** | 2 hours | Enjoy a contemplative walk south along the canal from Ginkaku-ji. |\\n| Evening | Dinner near the Path or back in Gion | 2.5 hours | **Dining:** Look for high-quality Tofu or *Kaiseki* (if budget permits) to reflect the refined nature of Higashiyama. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | Bus to Kiyomizu-dera, then walking between all sites today. | | |\\n\\n### Day 6: Mountain Spirits & Forest Hikes (Optional Day Trip: Kurama/Kibune)\\n\\n*This day requires slightly more travel time but offers a significant nature and spiritual contrast to the city.*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Travel to Kurama via Eiden Railway | 1.5 hours | Take the scenic Eiden Line north from Demachiyanagi Station. |\\n| Late Morning | **Kurama-dera Temple** | 2 hours | Hike up the steep path (or take the cable car) to this mountain temple, associated with Tengu folklore. |\\n| Lunch | Kibune Area | 1.5 hours | **Dining:** Head down the mountain trail toward Kibune. In summer, try *Kawadoko* dining (platforms built over the river). |\\n| Afternoon | **Kifune Shrine** | 2 hours | Famous for its striking red staircase and water fortune-telling slips (*Omikuji*). A lovely, atmospheric shrine. |\\n| Late Afternoon | Return Journey to Kyoto | 1.5 hours | Relaxing train ride back to the city. |\\n| Evening | Farewell Dinner - Splurge Option | 3 hours | **Dining:** Choose a special Kyoto cuisine you haven't tried yet, perhaps high-end *Wagyu* or *Kaiseki* to celebrate the trip. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | Eiden Railway (use ICOCA or a dedicated day pass). Walking/hiking between Kurama and Kibune. | | |\\n\\n### Day 7: Reflection & Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Last Temple Visit or Shopping | 2 hours | **Suggestion:** Revisit a favorite spot briefly, or visit a smaller site like **Chion-in Temple** (massive Sanmon gate). |\\n| Late Morning | Souvenir Shopping & Post Office | 1.5 hours | Final chance for *matcha*, artisanal goods, or sending postcards. |\\n| Lunch | Quick, favorite Kyoto meal | 1 hour | **Dining:** Your favorite simple meal from the week (e.g., ramen, tempura). |\\n| Afternoon | Travel to Airport/Next Destination | Varies | Ensure adequate time for travel to Kansai (KIX) or Itami (ITM) airports, typically requiring the Haruka Express or Limousine Bus from Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | IC Card usage for final transit. | | |\\n\\n    OPTIMIZATION TIPS: [your money-saving and practical tips here]\\n\\n    TRAVEL ESSENTIALS: [packing and preparation advice here]\\n\\n    BACKUP PLANS: [alternative options and contingencies here]\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"TravelOptimizerAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that optimizes travel plans with practical advice and alternatives\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]}}}},\"expected_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-f2ef503f-ae2d-4fe2-b8d4-40c7327c0051\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"As your TravelOptimizerAgent, I have integrated crucial money-saving strategies, specific packing lists, contingency plans, and essential local resources into your detailed 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary.\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary: Kyoto (Optimized)\\n\\nThis itinerary is designed for a comprehensive cultural immersion in Kyoto, balancing iconic historical sites with intimate experiences of Zen, tea traditions, and natural beauty.\\n\\n**Best Time to Travel:** Spring (Cherry Blossoms) or Autumn (Fall Foliage).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Area\\n\\nFor a first-time deep dive focusing on culture and accessibility, consider staying in or near:\\n\\n1.  **Higashiyama (East Kyoto):** Excellent access to Kiyomizu-dera, Gion, and the Philosopher's Path. Very atmospheric.\\n2.  **Central Kyoto (Near Karasuma/Kawaramachi Stations):** Best for transportation links (subway and buses) and modern dining/shopping convenience.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Day-by-Day Schedule (With Optimizations)\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & The Iconic East (Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Arrival at KIX/ITM, Transfer to Kyoto, Check-in | **BUDGET TIP:** If using KIX, the *Haruka Express* is fast but expensive. Consider the cheaper **JR Local Rapid Service** followed by a transfer, or the **Airport Limousine Bus** for direct, often cheaper service to Kyoto Station. |\\n| Lunch | Simple Udon or Soba near your hotel. | **MONEY SAVER:** Avoid restaurants directly facing major tourist spots (like the street leading to Kiyomizu-dera). Walk 2-3 blocks away for better value. |\\n| Afternoon | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | **CULTURAL DO\u2019s/DON\u2019Ts:** Use the purification fountain (Temizuya) correctly: ladle water, wash left hand, then right, pour water into your cupped left hand to rinse the ladle handle, then discard water. |\\n| Late Afternoon | Stroll **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** streets | **PACKING:** Wear comfortable, slip-on shoes. You will encounter many steep, uneven stone steps and some shops/temples require shoe removal. |\\n| Dinner | **Higashiyama Dinner** | **BACKUP:** If Pontocho/Gion is booked, try the dining options inside the Shijo Kawaramachi department stores (like Takashimaya or Daimaru) for high-quality but cheaper set menus at lunchtime or early dinner. |\\n\\n### Day 2: Zen, Tea, and Geiko Culture\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Traditional Tea Ceremony Experience** | **MONEY SAVER:** Book a group ceremony rather than a private one. Look for ceremonies held in public community centers or smaller temples rather than high-end tourist venues for savings of 30-50%. |\\n| Lunch | Lunch in Gion | **LOCAL APP:** Use **Tabelog** (Japan's primary restaurant review site) to find highly-rated, lower-priced lunch sets (*teishoku*) in the Gion area. |\\n| Afternoon | **Kodai-ji Temple** | **CULTURAL DON\u2019T:** Do not use flash photography anywhere inside temple buildings, even if it's not explicitly forbidden, as it degrades the art and disturbs visitors. |\\n| Evening | **Gion District Walk** | **CRUCIAL DON'T:** Never attempt to stop, touch, or photograph a Geiko/Maiko if they are clearly rushing. Respect their work commute. Stay on public sidewalks. |\\n| Dinner | **Pontocho Alley Dining** | **BACKUP PLAN:** If Pontocho is too busy or expensive, explore the smaller side alleys branching off Shijo Dori for less crowded *yakitori* joints. |\\n\\n### Day 3: Golden Splendor and Contemplation\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | **MONEY SAVER:** Entry fees are fixed, but arriving right at opening (9 AM) avoids paying for entry when the sun is harsh and crowds are thick, maximizing the visual experience for the standard fee. |\\n| Transit | Travel to Ryoan-ji | **TRANSPORT TIP:** Purchase an **ICOCA/Suica Card** immediately. This refillable card streamlines bus/subway travel immensely\u2014no need to fumble for exact change or bus passes daily. |\\n| Afternoon | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | **CULTURAL DO:** Sit quietly for at least 10 minutes at the viewing platform. The garden is designed for quiet contemplation; rushing it defeats the purpose of this Zen stop. |\\n| Late Afternoon | **Nishijin Textile Center (Optional)** | **BUDGET ALTERNATIVE:** If skipping the center, visit a small, independent craft shop in the Nishijin area to see artisans at work without the tourist shop markup. |\\n| Dinner | **Central Kyoto/Nishiki Market Area** | **MONEY SAVER:** Nishiki Market vendors often sell ready-to-eat foods (pickles, mochi, skewers). Grab a selection of these for a delicious, varied, and cheap dinner feast. |\\n\\n### Day 4: The Thousand Gates of Inari\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine** | **BACKUP PLAN (Heat/Rain):** If hiking in extreme heat or heavy rain, only go up to the first major junction (Yotsutsuji intersection) for the famous view, then descend. The upper mountain paths can be exposed. |\\n| Lunch | **Inari Street Food** | **CULTURAL DO:** Trying *Kitsune Udon* (udon noodles with sweet fried tofu) is highly recommended here, as the fox (*kitsune*) is the messenger of the Inari deity. |\\n| Afternoon | Return to Central Kyoto & Rest | **PACKING:** Bring moisture-wicking socks and a small towel. You will sweat while hiking Inari, and local washrooms/sento often charge for towel rental. |\\n| Evening | **Local Izakaya Experience** | **CULTURAL DO:** When ordering at an Izakaya, expect a small cover charge called *ot\u014dshi* or *tsukidashi*\u2014this is mandatory and includes a small appetizer. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Arashiyama Nature Immersion\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Travel to Arashiyama & **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | **MONEY SAVER:** Tenry\u016b-ji has two entry fees: the main hall/garden and the separate Bamboo Grove access. If you are on a tight budget, skip the main hall but walk through the famous **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** (Day 5 PM activity) which is free to access from the back paths. |\\n| Lunch | Riverside Dining | **BUDGET TIP:** Buy high-quality *bento* boxes from a department store basement (*depachika*) near Kyoto Station before heading out, and have a scenic picnic lunch by the river. |\\n| Afternoon | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | **LOCAL RESOURCE:** Download an offline map of Arashiyama. The paths diverge, and good signage is sometimes lacking deeper in the woods. |\\n| Evening | **Relaxation / Onsen** | **PACKING/CULTURAL NOTE:** If visiting a public bath/onsen, you must be comfortable bathing nude (separately for men/women). No tattoos are allowed in many traditional establishments\u2014research tattoo-friendly options like **Tendon no Yu** if this is a concern. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Shogun Power and Secluded Zen\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Nijo-jo Castle** | **CULTURAL DON'T:** Do not step on the edges of the \\\"nightingale floors\\\" as they are more likely to squeak when stepped on near the perimeter. Walk carefully in the center if possible. |\\n| Afternoon | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex** | **ZEN TIP:** Select *one* sub-temple (e.g., Koto-in) to focus on deeply, as paying entry fees for too many small temples drains the budget quickly. The larger Daitoku-ji grounds are free to wander. |\\n| Evening | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku-no-michi) Walk** | **BACKUP PLAN (Rain):** If it is raining heavily, substitute this walk with a visit to the **Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design** near Miyako Messe for an indoor cultural experience. |\\n| Dinner | **Near Demachiyanagi or Imadegawa** | **MONEY SAVER:** This university-adjacent area (near Kyoto University) has many excellent, cheap, and authentic restaurants catering to students. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Final Masterpieces & Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple** | **CULTURAL DO:** Photography is strictly forbidden inside. Before entering, pause outside to meditate on the sheer scale of the statues; this helps prepare your mind for the overwhelming experience inside. |\\n| Midday | **Final Reflection Spot (Honen-in Temple)** | **PACKING:** Pick up high-quality, long-lasting souvenirs like Kyoto incense (*Oko*) or preserved tea leaves today rather than waiting until the station. |\\n| Lunch | **Kyoto Station Area** | **BUDGET TIP:** Head to the Isetan department store food halls (*depachika*) around 1 PM. Some high-end deli/bakery items are deeply discounted near closing time (usually 6 PM onwards, check local schedules). |\\n| Afternoon | Souvenir Shopping & Departure Prep | **LOCAL APP:** Use **Google Maps** for real-time navigation to the airport transfer point, confirming the final platform/gate number for the Haruka Express or Shinkansen. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Optimization Deep Dive\\n\\n### \ud83d\udcb0 Money-Saving Tips & Budget Alternatives\\n\\n1.  **Transportation Flexibility:** Rely primarily on walking and the efficient bus network. Purchase the **Kyoto City Bus One-Day Pass (700 JPY)** only if you plan 3 or more bus rides. For days using the JR lines (Inari, Arashiyama), an ICOCA/Suica card is best.\\n2.  **Temple Passes:** Most major temples charge between 400\u2013600 JPY entry. If you find yourself consistently skipping afternoon visits due to fatigue or budget strain, prioritize only the UNESCO sites (Kiyomizu, Kinkaku-ji, Tenryu-ji) to save entry fees.\\n3.  **Dining Strategy:** Embrace *Konbini* (convenience stores like 7-Eleven, Lawson, Family Mart) for excellent, fresh breakfasts (Onigiri, Sandwiches) for under 400 JPY. For dinner, prioritize lunch sets (*teishoku*) which offer the same quality as dinner but at half the price.\\n4.  **Water:** Carry a reusable water bottle. Vending machines are everywhere (and cheaper than buying bottled water in stores), and many temples have public water sources for refilling (check signage).\\n\\n### \ud83e\uddf3 Packing Recommendations (Kyoto Specific)\\n\\n*   **Comfortable Footwear:** Non-negotiable. You will easily walk 8-10 miles daily over uneven paving, gravel, and stairs.\\n*   **Layered Clothing:** Evenings can be cool, especially near the rivers or when entering shady temple halls, even if the day is warm. Pack light, breathable layers.\\n*   **Small, Foldable Umbrella/Rain Shell:** Essential, especially during the rainy season (June) or unpredictable shoulder seasons.\\n*   **Small Gift (Omiyage):** If visiting a private tea ceremony or seeking specific artisanal services, having a small, attractively packaged souvenir from your home country (or a standard Japanese gift item) is a very polite gesture.\\n*   **Portable Charger:** Early mornings and late evenings mean relying heavily on navigation apps; keep your phone charged.\\n\\n### \ud83c\udf27\ufe0f Backup Plans for Weather or Unexpected Situations\\n\\n| Situation | Backup Activity / Contingency Plan |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **Heavy Rain/Typhoon Warning** | **Day 5/6 Substitute:** Visit the **Kyoto International Manga Museum** (Central Kyoto), explore the massive underground shopping malls around Kyoto Station, or spend a long afternoon in the **Kyoto National Museum** or the **Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design**. |\\n| **Extreme Heat (Summer)** | Focus on sites with excellent air conditioning (Nijo-jo, Museums). Schedule Fushimi Inari for pre-sunrise (5:30 AM start) and retreat indoors between 11 AM and 3 PM. Utilize public transport heavily. |\\n| **Unexpected Temple Closure** | Many small temples close for maintenance or private ceremonies. **Backup Plan:** Head to the nearby **Shinto Shrines** (which often have different opening hours) or explore local, unnamed neighborhood temples\u2014these are often the most authentic finds. |\\n| **Transportation Strikes/Delays** | If the JR line to Arashiyama is delayed, use the **Randen Tram** instead. It\u2019s slower but offers a lovely, scenic ride through older districts. |\\n\\n### \ud83d\udcf1 Local Apps, Websites, or Resources to Download\\n\\n1.  **Google Maps:** Essential for bus routes, walking times, and train schedules. Turn on \\\"Public Transit\\\" layers.\\n2.  **Japan Transit Planner (Jorudan) or Navitime:** Excellent secondary resources for complex train routes involving multiple carriers (e.g., Shinkansen connections).\\n3.  **Tabelog:** For reliable restaurant reviews and finding local gems away from tourist traps.\\n4.  **Google Translate:** Download the Japanese language pack for offline translation, particularly useful for reading menus or signs.\\n5.  **ICOCA/Suica App:** If you use a digital wallet on your phone, you can load and manage your transport card digitally, eliminating the need for a physical card.\\n\\n### \u26e9\ufe0f Cultural Do's & Don'ts for Respectful Travel\\n\\n| Do | Don't |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **DO** use quiet, respectful tones inside temples, shrines, and on public transport. | **DON'T** eat, drink, or smoke while walking down the historic streets (Sannenzaka, Gion). Find a designated spot. |\\n| **DO** learn a few key phrases: *Arigatou gozaimasu* (Thank you very much) and *Sumimasen* (Excuse me/Sorry). | **DON'T** take pictures of people without asking, especially Geiko/Maiko or people performing religious rites. |\\n| **DO** accept business cards (if exchanged) with both hands and examine them briefly before putting them away respectfully. | **DON'T** leave trash behind. Carry a small plastic bag for wrappers and bottles until you find a trash can. |\\n| **DO** remove your shoes promptly when entering homes, certain temples, and traditional restaurants/tea houses. | **DON'T** point with your index finger; use an open hand gesture instead. |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"tool_uses\":[],\"tool_responses\":[],\"intermediate_responses\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640594.995979,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":null},\"eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":1.0,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":0.7,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.6011778563015313,\"eval_status\":2,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}]}],\"session_id\":\"___eval___session___31312c09-05ca-40fb-b549-2c75a596e1dd\",\"session_details\":{\"id\":\"___eval___session___31312c09-05ca-40fb-b549-2c75a596e1dd\",\"app_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent\",\"user_id\":\"user\",\"state\":{\"travel_itinerary\":\"## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto: Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary is structured to group activities by geographic location to minimize travel time, maximizing your immersion in Kyoto's temples, traditions, and natural beauty.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Suggested Accommodation Area\\n\\nFor a cultural deep-dive, consider staying in the **Higashiyama (Eastern Kyoto)** or **Central Kyoto (near Kawaramachi/Gion)** districts. These areas offer excellent access to historic sites, dining, and a wonderful atmosphere for evening walks.\\n\\n### Estimated Budget Summary (Per Person, Excluding Flights)\\n\\n| Expense Category | Estimated Daily Cost (JPY) | Estimated Total (7 Days, JPY) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation** (Mid-range hotel/Ryokan) | \u00a515,000 \u2013 \u00a525,000 | \u00a5105,000 \u2013 \u00a5175,000 | Varies significantly based on quality and season. |\\n| **Food & Dining** (Including one nice meal) | \u00a56,000 \u2013 \u00a510,000 | \u00a542,000 \u2013 \u00a570,000 | Assumes a mix of quick lunches and moderate dinners. |\\n| **Local Transport** (Bus/Train/IC Card use) | \u00a51,000 \u2013 \u00a51,500 | \u00a57,000 \u2013 \u00a510,500 | Includes potential bus day passes. |\\n| **Entrance Fees** (Temples/Museums) | \u00a52,000 \u2013 \u00a53,000 | \u00a514,000 \u2013 \u00a521,000 | Based on visiting 2-3 paid sites daily. |\\n| **Experiences** (Tea Ceremony, etc.) | N/A (Budgeted below) | \u00a510,000 \u2013 \u00a515,000 | Estimate for one structured experience. |\\n| **Contingency/Shopping** | \u00a53,000+ | \u00a521,000+ | For souvenirs, snacks, and incidentals. |\\n| **TOTAL ESTIMATE** | **\u00a527,000 \u2013 \u00a540,000+** | **\u00a5199,000 \u2013 \u00a5291,500+** | *(Approx. $1,300 - $1,900 USD based on current exchange rates)* |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Detailed Daily Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & Golden Zenith (North/West Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Arrival, Check-in, & Travel Setup | 3 hours | Settle into accommodation. Purchase an ICOCA card if you don't have one. |\\n| Lunch | Local Udon/Soba near your hotel | 1 hour | Quick, comforting start. |\\n| Afternoon | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | 1.5 hours | Visit mid-afternoon for good light, but note crowds are highest here. |\\n| Late Afternoon | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | 1.5 hours | Contemplate the famous Zen rock garden. Perfect contrast to Kinkaku-ji\u2019s opulence. |\\n| Evening | Dinner in Central Kyoto (e.g., Pontocho Alley) | 2.5 hours | **Dining:** Explore Pontocho for atmospheric dining along the Kamo River (reservations recommended). |\\n| **Transport Focus** | Primarily Bus (from Central Kyoto to Kinkaku-ji/Ryoan-ji and back). | | |\\n\\n### Day 2: Arashiyama Bamboo & Zen Gardens (West Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Early Morning | Travel to Arashiyama & **Bamboo Grove** | 3 hours (incl. travel) | **Crucial: Arrive before 8:30 AM** to experience the grove without massive crowds. |\\n| Late Morning | **Tenryu-ji Temple** | 2 hours | Explore the vast, stunning UNESCO gardens. |\\n| Lunch | **Shojin Ryori (Zen Cuisine)** | 1.5 hours | **Dining:** Try *Shigetsu* within Tenryu-ji for an authentic vegetarian temple lunch experience (book ahead). |\\n| Afternoon | **Togetsukyo Bridge & River Walk** | 1.5 hours | Relaxing nature walk along the Katsura River. |\\n| Late Afternoon | Return to Kyoto & Relaxation | 1 hour | Travel back to your base area. |\\n| Evening | Dinner in Nishijin or near your hotel | 2 hours | **Dining:** Explore local neighborhood eateries for Yakitori or casual Japanese fare. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | JR Sagano Line or Randen Tram to Arashiyama. | | |\\n\\n### Day 3: Thousand Gates & Autumnal Grandeur (South Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Very Early AM | Travel to **Fushimi Inari Taisha** | 3 hours (incl. travel/hike) | **Crucial: Arrive before 7:30 AM.** Hike the torii gates trail as far as you feel comfortable for solitude. |\\n| Late Morning | Descend & Explore the lower shrine areas | 1 hour | Purchase an *omamori* (charm) or try the Fox-shaped fortune slips. |\\n| Lunch | Inari Area Street Food | 1 hour | **Dining:** Try *Inari Sushi* (rice stuffed tofu pockets) or grilled sparrows (if adventurous) near the shrine entrance. |\\n| Afternoon | **Tofuku-ji Temple** | 2 hours | Famous for its massive Sanmon Gate and beautiful, sweeping views (especially autumn leaves). |\\n| Late Afternoon | Travel & Tea Prep | 1.5 hours | Head back toward Central Kyoto. Prepare for your cultural deep-dive tomorrow. |\\n| Evening | Dinner in Gion area | 2.5 hours | **Dining:** Look for a modest restaurant in Gion, perhaps trying *obanzai* (Kyoto home cooking). Be respectful if spotting Geiko/Maiko. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | JR Nara Line to Fushimi Inari and Tofuku-ji. | | |\\n\\n### Day 4: Deep Dive into Tradition & Art (Central/East Kyoto Preparation)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Tea Ceremony Experience (*Chado*)** | 2 hours | **Activity Focus:** Book a highly rated, structured ceremony in advance (e.g., in the Gion or Higashiyama area). Focus on etiquette. |\\n| Late Morning | **Kennin-ji Temple** | 1.5 hours | Kyoto\u2019s oldest Zen temple. Admire the twin dragon ceiling painting in the Dharma Hall. |\\n| Lunch | Near Kennin-ji/Gion | 1 hour | **Dining:** A light lunch or coffee/tea break to digest the morning\u2019s ceremony. |\\n| Afternoon | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple** | 1.5 hours | Awe-inspiring hall housing 1001 Kannon statues. Photography is strictly prohibited inside. |\\n| Late Afternoon | Museum/Gallery Visit (Optional) | 1.5 hours | If time allows, visit the Kyoto National Museum nearby for context on the art you\u2019ve seen. |\\n| Evening | Explore Nishiki Market (for pre-dinner grazing) & Dinner | 3 hours | **Dining:** Explore **Nishiki Market** (closes around 5-6 PM for shopping, but nearby restaurants stay open). Find dinner in the surrounding Kawaramachi area. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | Mostly walking and short bus hops within Central/Southern Kyoto. | | |\\n\\n### Day 5: Eastern Hills, History, and Views (Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Early Morning | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | 2 hours | Arrive early (opens at 6 AM) to enjoy the famous wooden stage before the large crowds arrive. |\\n| Late Morning | **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka Streets** | 2 hours | Wander and shop these beautifully preserved, historic cobblestone streets leading up to/down from Kiyomizu-dera. Look for Kiyomizu-yaki pottery. |\\n| Lunch | Near Kodai-ji or Yasaka Shrine | 1 hour | **Dining:** Grab street snacks (like *yatsuhashi* sweets) or a small caf\u00e9 lunch. |\\n| Afternoon | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | 1.5 hours | Visit this Zen temple known for its stunning sand garden and moss garden. |\\n| Late Afternoon | **Philosopher\u2019s Path (Tetsugaku no Michi)** | 2 hours | Enjoy a contemplative walk south along the canal from Ginkaku-ji. |\\n| Evening | Dinner near the Path or back in Gion | 2.5 hours | **Dining:** Look for high-quality Tofu or *Kaiseki* (if budget permits) to reflect the refined nature of Higashiyama. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | Bus to Kiyomizu-dera, then walking between all sites today. | | |\\n\\n### Day 6: Mountain Spirits & Forest Hikes (Optional Day Trip: Kurama/Kibune)\\n\\n*This day requires slightly more travel time but offers a significant nature and spiritual contrast to the city.*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Travel to Kurama via Eiden Railway | 1.5 hours | Take the scenic Eiden Line north from Demachiyanagi Station. |\\n| Late Morning | **Kurama-dera Temple** | 2 hours | Hike up the steep path (or take the cable car) to this mountain temple, associated with Tengu folklore. |\\n| Lunch | Kibune Area | 1.5 hours | **Dining:** Head down the mountain trail toward Kibune. In summer, try *Kawadoko* dining (platforms built over the river). |\\n| Afternoon | **Kifune Shrine** | 2 hours | Famous for its striking red staircase and water fortune-telling slips (*Omikuji*). A lovely, atmospheric shrine. |\\n| Late Afternoon | Return Journey to Kyoto | 1.5 hours | Relaxing train ride back to the city. |\\n| Evening | Farewell Dinner - Splurge Option | 3 hours | **Dining:** Choose a special Kyoto cuisine you haven't tried yet, perhaps high-end *Wagyu* or *Kaiseki* to celebrate the trip. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | Eiden Railway (use ICOCA or a dedicated day pass). Walking/hiking between Kurama and Kibune. | | |\\n\\n### Day 7: Reflection & Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Time Req. | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Last Temple Visit or Shopping | 2 hours | **Suggestion:** Revisit a favorite spot briefly, or visit a smaller site like **Chion-in Temple** (massive Sanmon gate). |\\n| Late Morning | Souvenir Shopping & Post Office | 1.5 hours | Final chance for *matcha*, artisanal goods, or sending postcards. |\\n| Lunch | Quick, favorite Kyoto meal | 1 hour | **Dining:** Your favorite simple meal from the week (e.g., ramen, tempura). |\\n| Afternoon | Travel to Airport/Next Destination | Varies | Ensure adequate time for travel to Kansai (KIX) or Itami (ITM) airports, typically requiring the Haruka Express or Limousine Bus from Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Transport Focus** | IC Card usage for final transit. | | |\",\"__llm_request_key__\":\"b766cba6-f62c-452f-b66a-ab8faf35ea53\",\"destination_research\":\"Here is a comprehensive plan for your 7-day cultural deep-dive in Kyoto, focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\\n\\n***\\n\\n## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto\\n\\nKyoto, the ancient capital of Japan, is the perfect destination for a deep immersion into Japanese culture, history, and natural beauty.\\n\\n### Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto are **Spring (March to May)**, famous for cherry blossoms (late March to early April), and **Autumn (October to November)**, known for stunning fall foliage (peaking in November).\\n\\n*   **Spring (March - May):** Pleasant and comfortable weather, ideal for sightseeing. Expect average highs from 11\u201317\u00b0C in March up to 20\u00b0C in May. High season means bigger crowds and higher costs.\\n*   **Autumn (October - November):** Cool, comfortable, and often sunny weather, perfect for exploring gardens and temples. Average highs are around 22\u00b0C in October, dropping to 17\u00b0C in November. November rivals May as the best time for weather.\\n*   **Summer (June - August):** Hot and humid, with a rainy season from mid-June to late July.\\n*   **Winter (December - February):** Cold temperatures.\\n\\n***\\n\\n### Top Attractions & Must-See Locations (Thematic Grouping)\\n\\nFor a 7-day cultural deep-dive, focus on specific districts each day to maximize efficiency and immersion:\\n\\n#### **Day 1 & 2: Iconic Temples & Zen Focus (North/West Kyoto)**\\n*   **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion):** A UNESCO World Heritage Site, this shimmering pavilion reflected in a pond is iconic. Visit early to catch the best reflections.\\n*   **Ryoan-ji:** Famous for its mysterious and renowned Zen rock garden, perfect for contemplation.\\n*   **Tenryu-ji Temple (Arashiyama):** A UNESCO site with one of the city's oldest and most celebrated landscape gardens, especially beautiful in autumn. Consider trying *shojin ryori* (Zen Buddhist temple cuisine) at its restaurant, Shigetsu.\\n*   **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove:** Pair this natural wonder with your visit to Tenryu-ji.\\n\\n#### **Day 3 & 4: Southern Kyoto & Shinto Heritage**\\n*   **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine:** Famous for thousands of vibrant orange *torii* gates that snake up a mountain trail. Go beyond the main gates for a more peaceful hike. Visit early to avoid major crowds.\\n*   **Tofuku-ji Temple:** Known for its spectacular autumn colors and housing the oldest *sanmon* gate in Japan.\\n\\n#### **Day 5: Eastern Kyoto - Tradition & Nature Walk**\\n*   **Kiyomizu-dera Temple:** A revered World Heritage site known for its massive wooden veranda offering views over the city.\\n*   **Higashiyama District:** Wander the preserved historic streets around Kiyomizu-dera, browsing traditional shops.\\n*   **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku no Michi):** A lovely stone path that follows a canal, great for a nature walk. You can walk from Ginkaku-ji south towards Nanzen-ji or vice-versa.\\n*   **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion):** Features a famous sand garden, a contrast to Kinkaku-ji.\\n\\n#### **Day 6: Deeper Traditions & Central Sites**\\n*   **Tea Ceremony Experience:** Dedicate time to a proper *Chado* (Way of Tea) experience [see below].\\n*   **Kennin-ji Temple:** One of Kyoto's most important Zen temples, worth visiting for its historical significance and art.\\n*   **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple:** A must-see, housing 1001 statues of Kannon in its long wooden hall.\\n\\n#### **Day 7: Optional Day Trip or Cultural Deep Dive**\\n*   **Nature & Tradition Day Trip:** Consider a day trip to **Kurama and Kibune** in the northern mountains, accessible by the Eiden Railway, for forest hikes and beautiful nature temples like Kurama-dera.\\n*   **Alternative Day Trip:** Visit **Nara** to see Todai-ji Temple and interact with the wild deer.\\n\\n***\\n\\n### Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\n*   **Temple & Shrine Etiquette:** Dress respectfully (modest clothing) when visiting religious sites; no specific dress code is required, but avoid overly revealing attire. Remove hats before entering buildings, and do not take photos inside. At shrines, use the *temizuya* (purification fountain) to wash your hands and mouth before approaching the main hall.\\n*   **Tea Ceremony Etiquette:**\\n    *   Finish the seasonal sweet (*wagashi*) served first before beginning your tea. It is often eaten in three bites.\\n    *   When holding the tea bowl (*chawan*), support the base with your left hand and wrap your right hand around the side.\\n    *   Turn the bowl 180 degrees before drinking so the main design faces away from your mouth.\\n    *   Finish the matcha with a small, audible slurp as a compliment to the host.\\n    *   Wear clean socks, as shoes are removed before stepping onto *tatami* mats.\\n    *   Keep voices low; the ceremony is a meditative practice.\\n*   **General Conduct:** Speak in soft voices, as this reflects composure. Always be respectful when observing *Geiko* (Geisha in Kyoto) in districts like Gion; ask permission before taking photos.\\n\\n***\\n\\n### Transportation Options Within Kyoto\\n\\nKyoto has a good public transport system, but it is less comprehensively integrated than in cities like Tokyo, making a combination of methods best.\\n\\n*   **Bus:** The bus network is extensive and covers almost the entire city, making it convenient for reaching many major attractions. **Tip:** Enter at the back door and pay the fixed fare (or take a ticket if the fare varies) when exiting the front door.\\n*   **Subway & Trains:** Two subway lines offer efficient north-south and east-west travel. Local private train lines (like Keihan, Hankyu, Randen) are excellent for accessing specific areas like Arashiyama or Fushimi Inari.\\n*   **IC Cards & Passes:** Purchase an IC card (like ICOCA) for easy, near-universal payment on buses, subways, and trains. Day passes for buses/subways can save money if you plan extensive travel on those modes.\\n*   **Walking & Cycling:** Kyoto is relatively flat, making walking great for historic districts (like Higashiyama and Gion) and cycling a fantastic way to explore quieter areas.\\n*   **Taxis:** Plentiful and reasonably priced, especially for short trips or when traveling in a small group.\\n\\n***\\n\\n### Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** Kyoto is considered an **extremely safe city** with low crime rates. It is generally safe to walk alone at night.\\n*   **Emergencies:** Dial **119** for an ambulance or fire department. Dial **110** for the police.\\n*   **Water:** Tap water is safe to drink.\\n*   **Lost Items:** There is a high likelihood of lost items being returned.\\n*   **Nature Safety:** Be aware of occasional Asiatic black bear sightings, especially near mountains in the early morning or at dusk; do not panic if encountered.\\n*   **Travel Requirements (General):** For many nationalities, independent travel is possible with **no visa** required for short stays (up to 90 days) and **no COVID-19 protocols** for entry (no vaccination or test proof needed). Always verify current visa requirements with the nearest Japanese embassy before your trip, as conditions can change.\\n*   **Documentation:** Keep copies of important documents like your passport and travel insurance separate from the originals. Travel insurance is highly recommended for medical costs.\"},\"events\":[{\"model_version\":null,\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"grounding_metadata\":null,\"partial\":null,\"turn_complete\":null,\"finish_reason\":null,\"error_code\":null,\"error_message\":null,\"interrupted\":null,\"custom_metadata\":null,\"usage_metadata\":null,\"live_session_resumption_update\":null,\"input_transcription\":null,\"output_transcription\":null,\"avg_logprobs\":null,\"logprobs_result\":null,\"cache_metadata\":null,\"citation_metadata\":null,\"invocation_id\":\"e-e558685b-0159-467d-8417-803eedfb8189\",\"author\":\"user\",\"actions\":{\"skip_summarization\":null,\"state_delta\":{},\"artifact_delta\":{},\"transfer_to_agent\":null,\"escalate\":null,\"requested_auth_configs\":{},\"requested_tool_confirmations\":{},\"compaction\":null,\"end_of_agent\":null,\"agent_state\":null,\"rewind_before_invocation_id\":null},\"long_running_tool_ids\":null,\"branch\":null,\"id\":\"df14a364-bc91-4790-b7d9-ea0c2fac6199\",\"timestamp\":1763640706.044122},{\"model_version\":\"gemini-2.5-flash-lite-preview-09-2025\",\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Here is a comprehensive plan for your 7-day cultural deep-dive in Kyoto, focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\\n\\n***\\n\\n## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto\\n\\nKyoto, the ancient capital of Japan, is the perfect destination for a deep immersion into Japanese culture, history, and natural beauty.\\n\\n### Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto are **Spring (March to May)**, famous for cherry blossoms (late March to early April), and **Autumn (October to November)**, known for stunning fall foliage (peaking in November).\\n\\n*   **Spring (March - May):** Pleasant and comfortable weather, ideal for sightseeing. Expect average highs from 11\u201317\u00b0C in March up to 20\u00b0C in May. High season means bigger crowds and higher costs.\\n*   **Autumn (October - November):** Cool, comfortable, and often sunny weather, perfect for exploring gardens and temples. Average highs are around 22\u00b0C in October, dropping to 17\u00b0C in November. November rivals May as the best time for weather.\\n*   **Summer (June - August):** Hot and humid, with a rainy season from mid-June to late July.\\n*   **Winter (December - February):** Cold temperatures.\\n\\n***\\n\\n### Top Attractions & Must-See Locations (Thematic Grouping)\\n\\nFor a 7-day cultural deep-dive, focus on specific districts each day to maximize efficiency and immersion:\\n\\n#### **Day 1 & 2: Iconic Temples & Zen Focus (North/West Kyoto)**\\n*   **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion):** A UNESCO World Heritage Site, this shimmering pavilion reflected in a pond is iconic. Visit early to catch the best reflections.\\n*   **Ryoan-ji:** Famous for its mysterious and renowned Zen rock garden, perfect for contemplation.\\n*   **Tenryu-ji Temple (Arashiyama):** A UNESCO site with one of the city's oldest and most celebrated landscape gardens, especially beautiful in autumn. Consider trying *shojin ryori* (Zen Buddhist temple cuisine) at its restaurant, Shigetsu.\\n*   **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove:** Pair this natural wonder with your visit to Tenryu-ji.\\n\\n#### **Day 3 & 4: Southern Kyoto & Shinto Heritage**\\n*   **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine:** Famous for thousands of vibrant orange *torii* gates that snake up a mountain trail. Go beyond the main gates for a more peaceful hike. Visit early to avoid major crowds.\\n*   **Tofuku-ji Temple:** Known for its spectacular autumn colors and housing the oldest *sanmon* gate in Japan.\\n\\n#### **Day 5: Eastern Kyoto - Tradition & Nature Walk**\\n*   **Kiyomizu-dera Temple:** A revered World Heritage site known for its massive wooden veranda offering views over the city.\\n*   **Higashiyama District:** Wander the preserved historic streets around Kiyomizu-dera, browsing traditional shops.\\n*   **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku no Michi):** A lovely stone path that follows a canal, great for a nature walk. You can walk from Ginkaku-ji south towards Nanzen-ji or vice-versa.\\n*   **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion):** Features a famous sand garden, a contrast to Kinkaku-ji.\\n\\n#### **Day 6: Deeper Traditions & Central Sites**\\n*   **Tea Ceremony Experience:** Dedicate time to a proper *Chado* (Way of Tea) experience [see below].\\n*   **Kennin-ji Temple:** One of Kyoto's most important Zen temples, worth visiting for its historical significance and art.\\n*   **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple:** A must-see, housing 1001 statues of Kannon in its long wooden hall.\\n\\n#### **Day 7: Optional Day Trip or Cultural Deep Dive**\\n*   **Nature & Tradition Day Trip:** Consider a day trip to **Kurama and Kibune** in the northern mountains, accessible by the Eiden Railway, for forest hikes and beautiful nature temples like Kurama-dera.\\n*   **Alternative Day Trip:** Visit **Nara** to see Todai-ji Temple and interact with the wild deer.\\n\\n***\\n\\n### Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\n*   **Temple & Shrine Etiquette:** Dress respectfully (modest clothing) when visiting religious sites; no specific dress code is required, but avoid overly revealing attire. Remove hats before entering buildings, and do not take photos inside. At shrines, use the *temizuya* (purification fountain) to wash your hands and mouth before approaching the main hall.\\n*   **Tea Ceremony Etiquette:**\\n    *   Finish the seasonal sweet (*wagashi*) served first before beginning your tea. It is often eaten in three bites.\\n    *   When holding the tea bowl (*chawan*), support the base with your left hand and wrap your right hand around the side.\\n    *   Turn the bowl 180 degrees before drinking so the main design faces away from your mouth.\\n    *   Finish the matcha with a small, audible slurp as a compliment to the host.\\n    *   Wear clean socks, as shoes are removed before stepping onto *tatami* mats.\\n    *   Keep voices low; the ceremony is a meditative practice.\\n*   **General Conduct:** Speak in soft voices, as this reflects composure. Always be respectful when observing *Geiko* (Geisha in Kyoto) in districts like Gion; ask permission before taking photos.\\n\\n***\\n\\n### Transportation Options Within Kyoto\\n\\nKyoto has a good public transport system, but it is less comprehensively integrated than in cities like Tokyo, making a combination of methods best.\\n\\n*   **Bus:** The bus network is extensive and covers almost the entire city, making it convenient for reaching many major attractions. **Tip:** Enter at the back door and pay the fixed fare (or take a ticket if the fare varies) when exiting the front door.\\n*   **Subway & Trains:** Two subway lines offer efficient north-south and east-west travel. Local private train lines (like Keihan, Hankyu, Randen) are excellent for accessing specific areas like Arashiyama or Fushimi Inari.\\n*   **IC Cards & Passes:** Purchase an IC card (like ICOCA) for easy, near-universal payment on buses, subways, and trains. Day passes for buses/subways can save money if you plan extensive travel on those modes.\\n*   **Walking & Cycling:** Kyoto is relatively flat, making walking great for historic districts (like Higashiyama and Gion) and cycling a fantastic way to explore quieter areas.\\n*   **Taxis:** Plentiful and reasonably priced, especially for short trips or when traveling in a small group.\\n\\n***\\n\\n### Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** Kyoto is considered an **extremely safe city** with low crime rates. It is generally safe to walk alone at night.\\n*   **Emergencies:** Dial **119** for an ambulance or fire department. Dial **110** for the police.\\n*   **Water:** Tap water is safe to drink.\\n*   **Lost Items:** There is a high likelihood of lost items being returned.\\n*   **Nature Safety:** Be aware of occasional Asiatic black bear sightings, especially near mountains in the early morning or at dusk; do not panic if encountered.\\n*   **Travel Requirements (General):** For many nationalities, independent travel is possible with **no visa** required for short stays (up to 90 days) and **no COVID-19 protocols** for entry (no vaccination or test proof needed). Always verify current visa requirements with the nearest Japanese embassy before your trip, as conditions can change.\\n*   **Documentation:** Keep copies of important documents like your passport and travel insurance separate from the originals. Travel insurance is highly recommended for medical costs.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"grounding_metadata\":{\"google_maps_widget_context_token\":null,\"grounding_chunks\":[{\"maps\":null,\"retrieved_context\":null,\"web\":{\"domain\":null,\"title\":\"insidekyoto.com\",\"uri\":\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFOPoj6VO48wg7LovDjAifLF1kZ-IiFtb6LmItH8pEoPCZwW4M23V1t6GxoVgmNnlNyYyOI9EHrr7wdVer27G2YTDONCaGOF7uFRnWReJKSc4GvuN4DnVKJBoxwenCG-Awir8AUqmEUm3d_vTKv2CuwvGf5CP_jfMRe\"}},{\"maps\":null,\"retrieved_context\":null,\"web\":{\"domain\":null,\"title\":\"japanhighlights.com\",\"uri\":\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEgLR6mOvUxwqo70wYFK_6L7HsYrTwdbU_fBc3PWtuWouK0Yvi3d1s5cC8siwCAR2dJat6QaPGL6ShXQ2y_WYbMkKGiwiORP4gxO3nPU-W4ka1k7U5RSMDRW3Oxwb_QTIA6hEL5fQMyVPkGtIyUbstJk7WNtNnmJi70\"}},{\"maps\":null,\"retrieved_context\":null,\"web\":{\"domain\":null,\"title\":\"santorinidave.com\",\"uri\":\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFBvYbl7fHkJAxCXtGxmp9DK5a0WgHPjr1XJ3AtSUZPoVow39qQLQYZre0mYGm-UFw2auTrP9IOyru6R1gajzapNA--XrEU4xCBEzjR7VlOXt354vf_j8zATQNFXPQs-9rUG1KV0JsWAKsIe3Yf\"}},{\"maps\":null,\"retri